indices below\n249 |         `skip_num_points`. This is useful when computing transforms of new\n250 |         data where you'd like to keep the old data fixed.\n251 | \n252 |     verbose : int, default=False\n253 |         Verbosity level.\n254 | \n255 |     compute_error: bool, default=True\n256 |         If False, the kl_divergence is not computed and returns NaN.\n257 | \n258 |     num_threads : int, default=1\n259 |         Number of threads used to compute the gradient. This is set here to\n260 |         avoid calling _openmp_effective_n_threads for each gradient step.\n261 | \n262 |     Returns\n263 |     -------\n264 |     kl_divergence : float\n265 |         Kullback-Leibler divergence of p_ij and q_ij.\n266 | \n267 |     grad : ndarray of shape (n_params,)\n268 |         Unraveled gradient of the Kullback-Leibler divergence with respect to\n269 |         the embedding.\n270 |     \"\"\"\n271 |     params = params.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n272 |     X_embedded = params.reshape(n_samples, n_components)\n273 | \n274 |     val_P = P.data.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\n275 |     neighbors = P.indices.astype(np.int64, copy=False)\n276 |     indptr = P.indptr.astype(np.int64, copy=False)\n277 | \n278 |     grad = np.zeros(X_embedded.shape, dtype=np.float32)\n279 |     error = _barnes_hut_tsne.gradient(\n280 |         val_P,\n281 |         X_embedded,\n282 |         neighbors,\n283 |         indptr,\n284 |         grad,\n285 |         angle,\n286 |         n_components,\n287 |         verbose,\n288 |         dof=degrees_of_freedom,\n289 |         compute_error=compute_error,\n290 |         num_threads=num_threads,\n291 |     )\n292 |     c = 2.0 * (degrees_of_freedom + 1.0) / degrees_of_freedom\n293 |     grad = grad.ravel()\n294 |     grad *= c\n295 | \n296 |     return error, grad\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/neighbors/_base.py`.\nFound file `sklearn/multiclass.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to ge