 in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"datasets/_openml.py\", \"datasets/openml\", \"datasets/_openml.py datasets/openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_info_by_name`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n364 | def _get_data_info_by_name(\n365 |     name: str, version: Union[int, str], data_home: Optional[str]\n366 | ):\n367 |     \"\"\"\n368 |     Utilizes the openml dataset listing api to find a dataset by\n369 |     name/version\n370 |     OpenML api function:\n371 |     https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_list_data_name_data_name\n372 | \n373 |     Parameters\n374 |     ----------\n375 |     name : str\n376 |         name of the dataset\n377 | \n378 |     version : int or str\n379 |         If version is an integer, the exact name/version will be obtained from\n380 |         OpenML. If version is a string (value: \"active\") it will take the first\n381 |         version from OpenML that is annotated as active. Any other string\n382 |         values except \"active\" are treated as integer.\n383 | \n384 |     data_home : str or None\n385 |         Location to cache the response. None if no cache is required.\n386 | \n387 |     Returns\n388 |     -------\n389 |     first_dataset : json\n390 |         json representation of the first dataset object that adhired to the\n391 |         search criteria\n392 | \n393 |     \"\"\"\n394 |     if version == \"active\":\n395 |         # situation in which we return the oldest active version\n396 |         url = _SEARCH_NAME.format(name) + \"/status/active/\"\n397 |         error_msg = \"No active dataset {} found.\".format(name)\n398 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n399 |             url, error_msg, data_home=data_home\n400 |         )\n401 |         res = json_data['data']['dataset']\n402 |         if len(res) > 1:\n403 |             warn(\"Multiple active versions of the dataset matching the name\"\n404 |                  \" {name} exist. Versions may be fundamentally different, \"\n405 |                  \"returning version\"\n406 |                  \" {version}.\".format(name=name, version=res[0]['version']))\n407 |         return res[0]\n408 | \n409 |     # an integer version has been provided\n410 |     url = (_SEARCH_NAME + \"/data_version/{}\").format(name, version)\n411 |     try:\n412 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n413 |             url, error_message=None, data_home=data_home\n414 |         )\n415 |     except OpenMLError:\n416 |         # we can do this in 1 function call if OpenML does not require the\n417 |         # specification of the dataset status (i.e., return datasets with a\n418 |         # given name / version regardless of active, deactivated, etc. )\n419 |         # TODO: feature request OpenML.\n420 |         url += \"/status/deactivated\"\n421 |         error_msg = \"Dataset {} with version {} not found.\".format(name,\n422 |                                                                    version)\n423 |         json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n424 |             url, error_msg, data_home=data_home\n425 |         )\n426 | \n427 |     return json_data['data']['dataset'][0]\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_features`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n442 | def _get_data_features(\n443 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n444 | ) -> OpenmlFeaturesType:\n445 |     # OpenML function:\n446 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_features_id\n447 |     url = _DATA_FEATURES.format(data_id)\n448 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n449 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n450 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n451 |     )\n452 |     return json_data['data_features']['feature']\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_qualities`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n455 | def _get_data_qualities(\n456 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n457 | ) -> OpenmlQualitiesType:\n458 |     # OpenML API function:\n459 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_qualities_id\n460 |     url = _DATA_QUALITIES.format(data_id)\n461 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n462 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n463 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n464 |     )\n465 |     # the qualities might not be available, but we still try to process\n466 |     # the data\n467 |     return json_data.get('data_qualities', {}).get('quality', [])\n```\n\nFound file `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"datasets/_openml.py\", \"datasets/_openml.py datasets/openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_sparse_data_to_array`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n216 | def _sparse_data_to_array(\n217 |     arff_data: ArffSparseDataType, include_columns: List\n218 | ) -> np.ndarray:\n219 |     # turns the sparse data back into an array (can't use toarray() function,\n220 |     # as this does only work on numeric data)\n221 |     num_obs = max(arff_data[1]) + 1\n222 |     y_shape = (num_obs, len(include_columns))\n223 |     reindexed_columns = {column_idx: array_idx for array_idx, column_idx\n224 |                          in enumerate(include_columns)}\n225 |     # TODO: improve for efficiency\n226 |     y = np.empty(y_shape, dtype=np.float64)\n227 |     for val, row_idx, col_idx in zip(arff_data[0], arff_data[1], arff_data[2]):\n228 |         if col_idx in include_columns:\n229 |             y[row_idx, reindexed_columns[col_idx]] = val\n230 |     return y\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_num_samples`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n470 | def _get_num_samples(data_qualities: OpenmlQualitiesType) -> int:\n471 |     \"\"\"Get the number of samples from data qualities.\n472 | \n473 |     Parameters\n474 |     ----------\n475 |     data_qualities : list of dict\n476 |         Used to retrieve the number of instances (samples) in the dataset.\n477 | \n478 |     Returns\n479 |     -------\n480 |     n_samples : int\n481 |         The number of samples in the dataset or -1 if data qualities are\n482 |         unavailable.\n483 |     \"\"\"\n484 |     # If the data qualities are unavailable, we return -1\n485 |     default_n_samples = -1\n486 | \n487 |     qualities = {d['name']: d['value'] for d in data_qualities}\n488 |     return int(float(qualities.get('NumberOfInstances', default_n_samples)))\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"datasets/openml\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `examples/datasets/plot_random_multilabel_dataset.py`.\nFound file `examples/datasets/plot_random_dataset.py`.\nFound file `sklearn/datasets/_kddcup99.py`.\nFound file `sklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the external ARFF reader since it's involved in handling the downloaded data:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['sklearn/externals/_arff.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"sklearn/externals/_arff.py\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sklearn/externals/_arff.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/externals/_arff.py`.\n```\n   1 | # -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n   2 | # =============================================================================\n   3 | # Federal University of Rio Grande do Sul (UFRGS)\n   4 | # Connectionist Artificial Intelligence Laboratory (LIAC)\n   5 | # Renato de Pontes Pereira - rppereira@inf.ufrgs.br\n   6 | # =============================================================================\n   7 | # Copyright (c) 2011 Renato de Pontes Pereira, renato.ppontes at gmail dot com\n   8 | #\n   9 | # Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy\n  10 | # of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal\n  11 | # in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights\n  12 | # to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell\n  13 | # copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is\n  14 | # furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n  15 | #\n  16 | # The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in\n  17 | # all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n  18 | #\n  19 | # THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR\n  20 | # IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,\n  21 | # FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE\n  22 | # AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER\n  23 | # LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,\n  24 | # OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE\n  25 | # SOFTWARE.\n  26 | # =============================================================================\n  27 | \n  28 | '''\n  29 | The liac-arff module implements functions to read and write ARFF files in\n  30 | Python. It was created in the Connectionist Artificial Intelligence Laboratory\n  31 | (LIAC), which takes place at the Federal University of Rio Grande do Sul\n  32 | (UFRGS), in Brazil.\n  33 | \n  34 | ARFF (Attribute-Relation File Format) is an file format specially created for\n  35 | describe datasets which are commonly used for machine learning experiments and\n  36 | softwares. This file format was created to be used in Weka, the best\n  37 | representative software for machine learning automated experiments.\n  38 | \n  39 | An ARFF file can be divided into two sections: header and data. The Header\n  40 | describes the metadata of the dataset, including a general description of the\n  41 | dataset, its name and its attributes. The source below is an example of a\n  42 | header section in a XOR dataset::\n  43 | \n  44 |     %\n  45 |     % XOR Dataset\n  46 |     %\n  47 |     % Created by Renato Pereira\n  48 |     %            rppereira@inf.ufrgs.br\n  49 |     %            http://inf.ufrgs.br/~rppereira\n  50 |     %\n  51 |     %\n  52 |     @RELATION XOR\n  53 | \n  54 |     @ATTRIBUTE input1 REAL\n  55 |     @ATTRIBUTE input2 REAL\n  56 |     @ATTRIBUTE y REAL\n  57 | \n  58 | The Data section of an ARFF file describes the observations of the dataset, in\n  59 | the case of XOR dataset::\n  60 | \n  61 |     @DATA\n  62 |     0.0,0.0,0.0\n  63 |     0.0,1.0,1.0\n  64 |     1.0,0.0,1.0\n  65 |     1.0,1.0,0.0\n  66 |     %\n  67 |     %\n  68 |     %\n  69 | \n  70 | Notice that several lines are starting with an ``%`` symbol, denoting a\n  71 | comment, thus, lines with ``%`` at the beginning will be ignored, except by the\n  72 | description part at the beginning of the file. The declarations ``@RELATION``,\n  73 | ``@ATTRIBUTE``, and ``@DATA`` are all case insensitive and obligatory.\n  74 | \n  75 | For more information and details about the ARFF file description, consult\n  76 | http://www.cs.waikato.ac.nz/~ml/weka/arff.html\n  77 | \n  78 | \n  79 | ARFF Files in Python\n  80 | ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  81 | \n  82 | This module uses built-ins python objects to represent a deserialized ARFF\n  83 | file. A dictionary is used as the container of the data and metadata of ARFF,\n  84 | and have the following keys:\n  85 | \n  86 | - **description**: (OPTIONAL) a string with the description of the dataset.\n  87 | - **relation**: (OBLIGATORY) a string with the name of the dataset.\n  88 | - **attributes**: (OBLIGATORY) a list of attributes with the following\n  89 |   template::\n  90 | \n  91 |     (attribute_name, attribute_type)\n  92 | \n  93 |   the attribute_name is a string, and attribute_type must be an string\n  94 |   or a list of strings.\n  95 | - **data**: (OBLIGATORY) a list of data instances. Each data instance must be\n  96 |   a list with values, depending on the attributes.\n  97 | \n  98 | The above keys must follow the case which were described, i.e., the keys are\n  99 | case sensitive. The attribute type ``attribute_type`` must be one of these\n 100 | strings (they are not case sensitive): ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER``, ``REAL`` or\n 101 | ``STRING``. For nominal attributes, the ``attribute_type`` must be a list of\n 102 | strings.\n 103 | \n 104 | In this format, the XOR dataset presented above can be represented as a python\n 105 | object as::\n 106 | \n 107 |     xor_dataset = {\n 108 |         'description': 'XOR Dataset',\n 109 |         'relation': 'XOR',\n 110 |         'attributes': [\n 111 |             ('input1', 'REAL'),\n 112 |             ('input2', 'REAL'),\n 113 |             ('y', 'REAL'),\n 114 |         ],\n 115 |         'data': [\n 116 |             [0.0, 0.0, 0.0],\n 117 |             [0.0, 1.0, 1.0],\n 118 |             [1.0, 0.0, 1.0],\n 119 |             [1.0, 1.0, 0.0]\n 120 |         ]\n 121 |     }\n 122 | \n 123 | \n 124 | Features\n 125 | ~~~~~~~~\n 126 | \n 127 | This module provides several features, including:\n 128 | \n 129 | - Read and write ARFF files using python built-in structures, such dictionaries\n 130 |   and lists;\n 131 | - Supports `scipy.sparse.coo <http://docs.scipy\n 132 |   .org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.sparse.coo_matrix.html#scipy.sparse.coo_matrix>`_\n 133 |   and lists of dictionaries as used by SVMLight\n 134 | - Supports the following attribute types: NUMERIC, REAL, INTEGER, STRING, and\n 135 |   NOMINAL;\n 136 | - Has an interface similar to other built-in modules such as ``json``, or\n 137 |   ``zipfile``;\n 138 | - Supports read and write the descriptions of files;\n 139 | - Supports missing values and names with spaces;\n 140 | - Supports unicode values and names;\n 141 | - Fully compatible with Python 2.7+, Python 3.3+, pypy and pypy3;\n 142 | - Under `MIT License <http://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>`_\n 143 | \n 144 | '''\n 145 | __author__ = 'Renato de Pontes Pereira, Matthias Feurer, Joel Nothman'\n 146 | __author_email__ = ('renato.ppontes@gmail.com, '\n 147 |                     'feurerm@informatik.uni-freiburg.de, '\n 148 |                     'joel.nothman@gmail.com')\n 149 | __version__ = '2.4.0'\n 150 | \n 151 | import typing\n 152 | from typing import Optional, List, Dict, Any, Iterator, Union, Tuple\n 153 | \n 154 | import re\n 155 | import sys\n 156 | import csv\n 157 | \n 158 | # CONSTANTS ===================================================================\n 159 | _SIMPLE_TYPES = ['NUMERIC', 'REAL', 'INTEGER', 'STRING']\n 160 | \n 161 | _TK_DESCRIPTION = '%'\n 162 | _TK_COMMENT     = '%'\n 163 | _TK_RELATION    = '@RELATION'\n 164 | _TK_ATTRIBUTE   = '@ATTRIBUTE'\n 165 | _TK_DATA        = '@DATA'\n 166 | \n 167 | _RE_RELATION     = re.compile(r'^([^\\{\\}%,\\s]*|\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\')$', re.UNICODE)\n 168 | _RE_ATTRIBUTE    = re.compile(r'^(\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|[^\\{\\}%,\\s]*)\\s+(.+)$', re.UNICODE)\n 169 | _RE_TYPE_NOMINAL = re.compile(r'^\\{\\s*((\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|\\S*)\\s*,\\s*)*(\\\".*\\\"|\\'.*\\'|\\S*)\\s*\\}$', re.UNICODE)\n 170 | _RE_QUOTE_CHARS = re.compile(r'[\"\\'\\\\\\s%,\\000-\\031]', re.UNICODE)\n 171 | _RE_ESCAPE_CHARS = re.compile(r'(?=[\"\\'\\\\%])|[\\n\\r\\t\\000-\\031]')\n 172 | _RE_SPARSE_LINE = re.compile(r'^\\s*\\{.*\\}\\s*$', re.UNICODE)\n 173 | _RE_NONTRIVIAL_DATA = re.compile('[\"\\'{}\\\\s]', re.UNICODE)\n 174 | \n 175 | ArffDenseDataType = Iterator[List]\n 176 | ArffSparseDataType = Tuple[List, ...]\n 177 | \n 178 | \n 179 | if typing.TYPE_CHECKING:\n 180 |     # typing_extensions is available when mypy is installed\n 181 |     from typing_extensions import TypedDict\n 182 | \n 183 |     class ArffContainerType(TypedDict):\n 184 |         description: str\n 185 |         relation: str\n 186 |         attributes: List\n 187 |         data: Union[ArffDenseDataType, ArffSparseDataType]\n 188 | \n 189 | else:\n 190 |     ArffContainerType = Dict[str, Any]\n 191 | \n 192 | \n 193 | def _build_re_values():\n 194 |     quoted_re = r'''\n 195 |                     \"      # open quote followed by zero or more of:\n 196 |                     (?:\n 197 |                         (?<!\\\\)    # no additional backslash\n 198 |                         (?:\\\\\\\\)*  # maybe escaped backslashes\n 199 |                         \\\\\"        # escaped quote\n 200 |                     |\n 201 |                         \\\\[^\"]     # escaping a non-quote\n 202 |                     |\n 203 |                         [^\"\\\\]     # non-quote char\n 204 |                     )*\n 205 |                     \"      # close quote\n 206 |                     '''\n 207 |     # a value is surrounded by \" or by ' or contains no quotables\n 208 |     value_re = r'''(?:\n 209 |         %s|          # a value may be surrounded by \"\n 210 |         %s|          # or by '\n 211 |         [^,\\s\"'{}]+  # or may contain no characters requiring quoting\n 212 |         )''' % (quoted_re,\n 213 |                 quoted_re.replace('\"', \"'\"))\n 214 | \n 215 |     # This captures (value, error) groups. Because empty values are allowed,\n 216 |     # we cannot just look for empty values to handle syntax errors.\n 217 |     # We presume the line has had ',' prepended...\n 218 |     dense = re.compile(r'''(?x)\n 219 |         ,                # may follow ','\n 220 |         \\s*\n 221 |         ((?=,)|$|%(value_re)s)  # empty or value\n 222 |         |\n 223 |         (\\S.*)           # error\n 224 |         ''' % {'value_re': value_re})\n 225 | \n 226 |     # This captures (key, value) groups and will have an empty key/value\n 227 |     # in case of syntax errors.\n 228 |     # It does not ensure that the line starts with '{' or ends with '}'.\n 229 |     sparse = re.compile(r'''(?x)\n 230 |         (?:^\\s*\\{|,)   # may follow ',', or '{' at line start\n 231 |         \\s*\n 232 |         (\\d+)          # attribute key\n 233 |         \\s+\n 234 |         (%(value_re)s) # value\n 235 |         |\n 236 |         (?!}\\s*$)      # not an error if it's }$\n 237 |         (?!^\\s*{\\s*}\\s*$)  # not an error if it's ^{}$\n 238 |         \\S.*           # error\n 239 |         ''' % {'value_re': value_re})\n 240 |     return dense, sparse\n 241 | \n 242 | \n 243 | _RE_DENSE_VALUES, _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES = _build_re_values()\n 244 | \n 245 | \n 246 | _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP = {\n 247 |     '\\\\\\\\': '\\\\',\n 248 |     '\\\\\"': '\"',\n 249 |     \"\\\\'\": \"'\",\n 250 |     '\\\\t': '\\t',\n 251 |     '\\\\n': '\\n',\n 252 |     '\\\\r': '\\r',\n 253 |     '\\\\b': '\\b',\n 254 |     '\\\\f': '\\f',\n 255 |     '\\\\%': '%',\n 256 | }\n 257 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP = {chr(i): '\\\\%03o' % i for i in range(32)}\n 258 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP.update({v: k for k, v in _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP.items()})\n 259 | _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP[''] = '\\\\'\n 260 | _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP.update({'\\\\%d' % i: chr(i) for i in range(10)})\n 261 | \n 262 | \n 263 | def _escape_sub_callback(match):\n 264 |     s = match.group()\n 265 |     if len(s) == 2:\n 266 |         try:\n 267 |             return _ESCAPE_SUB_MAP[s]\n 268 |         except KeyError:\n 269 |             raise ValueError('Unsupported escape sequence: %s' % s)\n 270 |     if s[1] == 'u':\n 271 |         return unichr(int(s[2:], 16))\n 272 |     else:\n 273 |         return chr(int(s[1:], 8))\n 274 | \n 275 | \n 276 | def _unquote(v):\n 277 |     if v[:1] in ('\"', \"'\"):\n 278 |         return re.sub(r'\\\\([0-9]{1,3}|u[0-9a-f]{4}|.)', _escape_sub_callback,\n 279 |                       v[1:-1])\n 280 |     elif v in ('?', ''):\n 281 |         return None\n 282 |     else:\n 283 |         return v\n 284 | \n 285 | \n 286 | def _parse_values(s):\n 287 |     '''(INTERNAL) Split a line into a list of values'''\n 288 |     if not _RE_NONTRIVIAL_DATA.search(s):\n 289 |         # Fast path for trivial cases (unfortunately we have to handle missing\n 290 |         # values because of the empty string case :(.)\n 291 |         return [None if s in ('?', '') else s\n 292 |                 for s in next(csv.reader([s]))]\n 293 | \n 294 |     # _RE_DENSE_VALUES tokenizes despite quoting, whitespace, etc.\n 295 |     values, errors = zip(*_RE_DENSE_VALUES.findall(',' + s))\n 296 |     if not any(errors):\n 297 |         return [_unquote(v) for v in values]\n 298 |     if _RE_SPARSE_LINE.match(s):\n 299 |         try:\n 300 |             return {int(k): _unquote(v)\n 301 |                     for k, v in _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES.findall(s)}\n 302 |         except ValueError as exc:\n 303 |             # an ARFF syntax error in sparse data\n 304 |             for match in _RE_SPARSE_KEY_VALUES.finditer(s):\n 305 |                 if not match.group(1):\n 306 |                     raise BadLayout('Error parsing %r' % match.group())\n 307 |             raise BadLayout('Unknown parsing error')\n 308 |     else:\n 309 |         # an ARFF syntax error\n 310 |         for match in _RE_DENSE_VALUES.finditer(s):\n 311 |             if match.group(2):\n 312 |                 raise BadLayout('Error parsing %r' % match.group())\n 313 |         raise BadLayout('Unknown parsing error')\n 314 | \n 315 | \n 316 | DENSE = 0     # Constant value representing a dense matrix\n 317 | COO = 1       # Constant value representing a sparse matrix in coordinate format\n 318 | LOD = 2       # Constant value representing a sparse matrix in list of\n 319 |               # dictionaries format\n 320 | DENSE_GEN = 3 # Generator of dictionaries\n 321 | LOD_GEN = 4   # Generator of dictionaries\n 322 | _SUPPORTED_DATA_STRUCTURES = [DENSE, COO, LOD, DENSE_GEN, LOD_GEN]\n 323 | \n 324 | # =============================================================================\n 325 | \n 326 | # COMPATIBILITY WITH PYTHON 3 =================================================\n 327 | PY3 = sys.version_info[0] == 3\n 328 | if PY3:\n 329 |     unicode = str\n 330 |     basestring = str\n 331 |     xrange = range\n 332 |     unichr = chr\n 333 | # COMPABILITY WITH PYTHON 2 ===================================================\n 334 | # =============================================================================\n 335 | PY2 = sys.version_info[0] == 2\n 336 | if PY2:\n 337 |     from itertools import izip as zip\n 338 | \n 339 | # EXCEPTIONS ==================================================================\n 340 | class ArffException(Exception):\n 341 |     message : Optional[str] = None\n 342 | \n 343 |     def __init__(self):\n 344 |         self.line = -1\n 345 | \n 346 |     def __str__(self):\n 347 |         return self.message%self.line\n 348 | \n 349 | class BadRelationFormat(ArffException):\n 350 |     '''Error raised when the relation declaration is in an invalid format.'''\n 351 |     message = 'Bad @RELATION format, at line %d.'\n 352 | \n 353 | class BadAttributeFormat(ArffException):\n 354 |     '''Error raised when some attribute declaration is in an invalid format.'''\n 355 |     message = 'Bad @ATTRIBUTE format, at line %d.'\n 356 | \n 357 | class BadDataFormat(ArffException):\n 358 |     '''Error raised when some data instance is in an invalid format.'''\n 359 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 360 |         super(BadDataFormat, self).__init__()\n 361 |         self.message = (\n 362 |             'Bad @DATA instance format in line %d: ' +\n 363 |             ('%s' % value)\n 364 |         )\n 365 | \n 366 | class BadAttributeType(ArffException):\n 367 |     '''Error raised when some invalid type is provided into the attribute\n 368 |     declaration.'''\n 369 |     message = 'Bad @ATTRIBUTE type, at line %d.'\n 370 | \n 371 | class BadAttributeName(ArffException):\n 372 |     '''Error raised when an attribute name is provided twice the attribute\n 373 |     declaration.'''\n 374 | \n 375 |     def __init__(self, value, value2):\n 376 |         super(BadAttributeName, self).__init__()\n 377 |         self.message = (\n 378 |             ('Bad @ATTRIBUTE name %s at line' % value) +\n 379 |             ' %d, this name is already in use in line' +\n 380 |             (' %d.' % value2)\n 381 |         )\n 382 | \n 383 | class BadNominalValue(ArffException):\n 384 |     '''Error raised when a value in used in some data instance but is not\n 385 |     declared into it respective attribute declaration.'''\n 386 | \n 387 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 388 |         super(BadNominalValue, self).__init__()\n 389 |         self.message = (\n 390 |             ('Data value %s not found in nominal declaration, ' % value)\n 391 |             + 'at line %d.'\n 392 |         )\n 393 | \n 394 | class BadNominalFormatting(ArffException):\n 395 |     '''Error raised when a nominal value with space is not properly quoted.'''\n 396 |     def __init__(self, value):\n 397 |         super(BadNominalFormatting, self).__init__()\n 398 |         self.message = (\n 399 |             ('Nominal data value \"%s\" not properly quoted in line ' % value) +\n 400 |             '%d.'\n 401 |         )\n 402 | \n 403 | class BadNumericalValue(ArffException):\n 404 |     '''Error raised when and invalid numerical value is used in some data\n 405 |     instance.'''\n 406 |     message = 'Invalid numerical value, at line %d.'\n 407 | \n 408 | class BadStringValue(ArffException):\n 409 |     '''Error raise when a string contains space but is not quoted.'''\n 410 |     message = 'Invalid string value at line %d.'\n 411 | \n 412 | class BadLayout(ArffException):\n 413 |     '''Error raised when the layout of the ARFF file has something wrong.'''\n 414 |     message = 'Invalid layout of the ARFF file, at line %d.'\n 415 | \n 416 |     def __init__(self, msg=''):\n 417 |         super(BadLayout, self).__init__()\n 418 |         if msg:\n 419 |             self.message = BadLayout.message + ' ' + msg.replace('%', '%%')\n 420 | \n 421 | \n 422 | class BadObject(ArffException):\n 423 |     '''Error raised when the object representing the ARFF file has something\n 424 |     wrong.'''\n 425 |     def __init__(self, msg='Invalid object.'):\n 426 |         self.msg = msg\n 427 | \n 428 |     def __str__(self):\n 429 |         return '%s' % self.msg\n 430 | \n 431 | # =============================================================================\n 432 | \n 433 | # INTERNAL ====================================================================\n 434 | def _unescape_sub_callback(match):\n 435 |     return _UNESCAPE_SUB_MAP[match.group()]\n 436 | \n 437 | \n 438 | def encode_string(s):\n 439 |     if _RE_QUOTE_CHARS.search(s):\n 440 |         return u\"'%s'\" % _RE_ESCAPE_CHARS.sub(_unescape_sub_callback, s)\n 441 |     return s\n 442 | \n 443 | \n 444 | class EncodedNominalConversor(object):\n 445 |     def __init__(self, values):\n 446 |         self.values = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(values)}\n 447 |         self.values[0] = 0\n 448 | \n 449 |     def __call__(self, value):\n 450 |         try:\n 451 |             return self.values[value]\n 452 |         except KeyError:\n 453 |             raise BadNominalValue(value)\n 454 | \n 455 | \n 456 | class NominalConversor(object):\n 457 |     def __init__(self, values):\n 458 |         self.values = set(values)\n 459 |         self.zero_value = values[0]\n 460 | \n 461 |     def __call__(self, value):\n 462 |         if value not in self.values:\n 463 |             if value == 0:\n 464 |                 # Sparse decode\n 465 |                 # See issue #52: nominals should take their first value when\n 466 |                 # unspecified in a sparse matrix. Naturally, this is consistent\n 467 |                 # with EncodedNominalConversor.\n 468 |                 return self.zero_value\n 469 |             raise BadNominalValue(value)\n 470 |         return unicode(value)\n 471 | \n 472 | \n 473 | class DenseGeneratorData(object):\n 474 |     '''Internal helper class to allow for different matrix types without\n 475 |     making the code a huge collection of if statements.'''\n 476 | \n 477 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 478 |         for row in stream:\n 479 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 480 | \n 481 |             if isinstance(values, dict):\n 482 |                 if values and max(values) >= len(conversors):\n 483 |                     raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 484 |                 # XXX: int 0 is used for implicit values, not '0'\n 485 |                 values = [values[i] if i in values else 0 for i in\n 486 |                           xrange(len(conversors))]\n 487 |             else:\n 488 |                 if len(values) != len(conversors):\n 489 |                     raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 490 | \n 491 |             yield self._decode_values(values, conversors)\n 492 | \n 493 |     @staticmethod\n 494 |     def _decode_values(values, conversors):\n 495 |         try:\n 496 |             values = [None if value is None else conversor(value)\n 497 |                       for conversor, value\n 498 |                       in zip(conversors, values)]\n 499 |         except ValueError as exc:\n 500 |             if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 501 |                 raise BadNumericalValue()\n 502 |         return values\n 503 | \n 504 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 505 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes a line of data.\n 506 | \n 507 |         Data instances follow the csv format, i.e, attribute values are\n 508 |         delimited by commas. After converted from csv.\n 509 | \n 510 |         :param data: a list of values.\n 511 |         :param attributes: a list of attributes. Used to check if data is valid.\n 512 |         :return: a string with the encoded data line.\n 513 |         '''\n 514 |         current_row = 0\n 515 | \n 516 |         for inst in data:\n 517 |             if len(inst) != len(attributes):\n 518 |                 raise BadObject(\n 519 |                     'Instance %d has %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 520 |                      (current_row, len(inst), len(attributes))\n 521 |                 )\n 522 | \n 523 |             new_data = []\n 524 |             for value in inst:\n 525 |                 if value is None or value == u'' or value != value:\n 526 |                     s = '?'\n 527 |                 else:\n 528 |                     s = encode_string(unicode(value))\n 529 |                 new_data.append(s)\n 530 | \n 531 |             current_row += 1\n 532 |             yield u','.join(new_data)\n 533 | \n 534 | \n 535 | class _DataListMixin(object):\n 536 |     \"\"\"Mixin to return a list from decode_rows instead of a generator\"\"\"\n 537 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 538 |         return list(super(_DataListMixin, self).decode_rows(stream, conversors))\n 539 | \n 540 | \n 541 | class Data(_DataListMixin, DenseGeneratorData):\n 542 |     pass\n 543 | \n 544 | \n 545 | class COOData(object):\n 546 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 547 |         data, rows, cols = [], [], []\n 548 |         for i, row in enumerate(stream):\n 549 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 550 |             if not isinstance(values, dict):\n 551 |                 raise BadLayout()\n 552 |             if not values:\n 553 |                 continue\n 554 |             row_cols, values = zip(*sorted(values.items()))\n 555 |             try:\n 556 |                 values = [value if value is None else conversors[key](value)\n 557 |                           for key, value in zip(row_cols, values)]\n 558 |             except ValueError as exc:\n 559 |                 if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 560 |                     raise BadNumericalValue()\n 561 |                 raise\n 562 |             except IndexError:\n 563 |                 # conversor out of range\n 564 |                 raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 565 | \n 566 |             data.extend(values)\n 567 |             rows.extend([i] * len(values))\n 568 |             cols.extend(row_cols)\n 569 | \n 570 |         return data, rows, cols\n 571 | \n 572 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 573 |         num_attributes = len(attributes)\n 574 |         new_data = []\n 575 |         current_row = 0\n 576 | \n 577 |         row = data.row\n 578 |         col = data.col\n 579 |         data = data.data\n 580 | \n 581 |         # Check if the rows are sorted\n 582 |         if not all(row[i] <= row[i + 1] for i in xrange(len(row) - 1)):\n 583 |             raise ValueError(\"liac-arff can only output COO matrices with \"\n 584 |                              \"sorted rows.\")\n 585 | \n 586 |         for v, col, row in zip(data, col, row):\n 587 |             if row > current_row:\n 588 |                 # Add empty rows if necessary\n 589 |                 while current_row < row:\n 590 |                     yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 591 |                     new_data = []\n 592 |                     current_row += 1\n 593 | \n 594 |             if col >= num_attributes:\n 595 |                 raise BadObject(\n 596 |                     'Instance %d has at least %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 597 |                     (current_row, col + 1, num_attributes)\n 598 |                 )\n 599 | \n 600 |             if v is None or v == u'' or v != v:\n 601 |                 s = '?'\n 602 |             else:\n 603 |                 s = encode_string(unicode(v))\n 604 |             new_data.append(\"%d %s\" % (col, s))\n 605 | \n 606 |         yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 607 | \n 608 | class LODGeneratorData(object):\n 609 |     def decode_rows(self, stream, conversors):\n 610 |         for row in stream:\n 611 |             values = _parse_values(row)\n 612 | \n 613 |             if not isinstance(values, dict):\n 614 |                 raise BadLayout()\n 615 |             try:\n 616 |                 yield {key: None if value is None else conversors[key](value)\n 617 |                        for key, value in values.items()}\n 618 |             except ValueError as exc:\n 619 |                 if 'float: ' in str(exc):\n 620 |                     raise BadNumericalValue()\n 621 |                 raise\n 622 |             except IndexError:\n 623 |                 # conversor out of range\n 624 |                 raise BadDataFormat(row)\n 625 | \n 626 |     def encode_data(self, data, attributes):\n 627 |         current_row = 0\n 628 | \n 629 |         num_attributes = len(attributes)\n 630 |         for row in data:\n 631 |             new_data = []\n 632 | \n 633 |             if len(row) > 0 and max(row) >= num_attributes:\n 634 |                 raise BadObject(\n 635 |                     'Instance %d has %d attributes, expected %d' %\n 636 |                     (current_row, max(row) + 1, num_attributes)\n 637 |                 )\n 638 | \n 639 |             for col in sorted(row):\n 640 |                 v = row[col]\n 641 |                 if v is None or v == u'' or v != v:\n 642 |                     s = '?'\n 643 |                 else:\n 644 |                     s = encode_string(unicode(v))\n 645 |                 new_data.append(\"%d %s\" % (col, s))\n 646 | \n 647 |             current_row += 1\n 648 |             yield \" \".join([u\"{\", u','.join(new_data), u\"}\"])\n 649 | \n 650 | class LODData(_DataListMixin, LODGeneratorData):\n 651 |     pass\n 652 | \n 653 | \n 654 | def _get_data_object_for_decoding(matrix_type):\n 655 |     if matrix_type == DENSE:\n 656 |         return Data()\n 657 |     elif matrix_type == COO:\n 658 |         return COOData()\n 659 |     elif matrix_type == LOD:\n 660 |         return LODData()\n 661 |     elif matrix_type == DENSE_GEN:\n 662 |         return DenseGeneratorData()\n 663 |     elif matrix_type == LOD_GEN:\n 664 |         return LODGeneratorData()\n 665 |     else:\n 666 |         raise ValueError(\"Matrix type %s not supported.\" % str(matrix_type))\n 667 | \n 668 | def _get_data_object_for_encoding(matrix):\n 669 |     # Probably a scipy.sparse\n 670 |     if hasattr(matrix, 'format'):\n 671 |         if matrix.format == 'coo':\n 672 |             return COOData()\n 673 |         else:\n 674 |             raise ValueError('Cannot guess matrix format!')\n 675 |     elif isinstance(matrix[0], dict):\n 676 |         return LODData()\n 677 |     else:\n 678 |         return Data()\n 679 | \n 680 | # =============================================================================\n 681 | \n 682 | # ADVANCED INTERFACE ==========================================================\n 683 | class ArffDecoder(object):\n 684 |     '''An ARFF decoder.'''\n 685 | \n 686 |     def __init__(self):\n 687 |         '''Constructor.'''\n 688 |         self._conversors = []\n 689 |         self._current_line = 0\n 690 | \n 691 |     def _decode_comment(self, s):\n 692 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a comment line.\n 693 | \n 694 |         Comments are single line strings starting, obligatorily, with the ``%``\n 695 |         character, and can have any symbol, including whitespaces or special\n 696 |         characters.\n 697 | \n 698 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 699 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 700 | \n 701 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 702 |         :return: a string with the decoded comment.\n 703 |         '''\n 704 |         res = re.sub(r'^\\%( )?', '', s)\n 705 |         return res\n 706 | \n 707 |     def _decode_relation(self, s):\n 708 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a relation line.\n 709 | \n 710 |         The relation declaration is a line with the format ``@RELATION\n 711 |         <relation-name>``, where ``relation-name`` is a string. The string must\n 712 |         start with alphabetic character and must be quoted if the name includes\n 713 |         spaces, otherwise this method will raise a `BadRelationFormat` exception.\n 714 | \n 715 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 716 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 717 | \n 718 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 719 |         :return: a string with the decoded relation name.\n 720 |         '''\n 721 |         _, v = s.split(' ', 1)\n 722 |         v = v.strip()\n 723 | \n 724 |         if not _RE_RELATION.match(v):\n 725 |             raise BadRelationFormat()\n 726 | \n 727 |         res = unicode(v.strip('\"\\''))\n 728 |         return res\n 729 | \n 730 |     def _decode_attribute(self, s):\n 731 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes an attribute line.\n 732 | \n 733 |         The attribute is the most complex declaration in an arff file. All\n 734 |         attributes must follow the template::\n 735 | \n 736 |              @attribute <attribute-name> <datatype>\n 737 | \n 738 |         where ``attribute-name`` is a string, quoted if the name contains any\n 739 |         whitespace, and ``datatype`` can be:\n 740 | \n 741 |         - Numerical attributes as ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER`` or ``REAL``.\n 742 |         - Strings as ``STRING``.\n 743 |         - Dates (NOT IMPLEMENTED).\n 744 |         - Nominal attributes with format:\n 745 | \n 746 |             {<nominal-name1>, <nominal-name2>, <nominal-name3>, ...}\n 747 | \n 748 |         The nominal names follow the rules for the attribute names, i.e., they\n 749 |         must be quoted if the name contains whitespaces.\n 750 | \n 751 |         This method must receive a normalized string, i.e., a string without\n 752 |         padding, including the \"\\r\\n\" characters.\n 753 | \n 754 |         :param s: a normalized string.\n 755 |         :return: a tuple (ATTRIBUTE_NAME, TYPE_OR_VALUES).\n 756 |         '''\n 757 |         _, v = s.split(' ', 1)\n 758 |         v = v.strip()\n 759 | \n 760 |         # Verify the general structure of declaration\n 761 |         m = _RE_ATTRIBUTE.match(v)\n 762 |         if not m:\n 763 |             raise BadAttributeFormat()\n 764 | \n 765 |         # Extracts the raw name and type\n 766 |         name, type_ = m.groups()\n 767 | \n 768 |         # Extracts the final name\n 769 |         name = unicode(name.strip('\"\\''))\n 770 | \n 771 |         # Extracts the final type\n 772 |         if _RE_TYPE_NOMINAL.match(type_):\n 773 |             try:\n 774 |                 type_ = _parse_values(type_.strip('{} '))\n 775 |             except Exception:\n 776 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 777 |             if isinstance(type_, dict):\n 778 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 779 | \n 780 |         else:\n 781 |             # If not nominal, verify the type name\n 782 |             type_ = unicode(type_).upper()\n 783 |             if type_ not in ['NUMERIC', 'REAL', 'INTEGER', 'STRING']:\n 784 |                 raise BadAttributeType()\n 785 | \n 786 |         return (name, type_)\n 787 | \n 788 |     def _decode(self, s, encode_nominal=False, matrix_type=DENSE):\n 789 |         '''Do the job the ``encode``.'''\n 790 | \n 791 |         # Make sure this method is idempotent\n 792 |         self._current_line = 0\n 793 | \n 794 |         # If string, convert to a list of lines\n 795 |         if isinstance(s, basestring):\n 796 |             s = s.strip('\\r\\n ').replace('\\r\\n', '\\n').split('\\n')\n 797 | \n 798 |         # Create the return object\n 799 |         obj: ArffContainerType = {\n 800 |             u'description': u'',\n 801 |             u'relation': u'',\n 802 |             u'attributes': [],\n 803 |             u'data': []\n 804 |         }\n 805 |         attribute_names = {}\n 806 | \n 807 |         # Create the data helper object\n 808 |         data = _get_data_object_for_decoding(matrix_type)\n 809 | \n 810 |         # Read all lines\n 811 |         STATE = _TK_DESCRIPTION\n 812 |         s = iter(s)\n 813 |         for row in s:\n 814 |             self._current_line += 1\n 815 |             # Ignore empty lines\n 816 |             row = row.strip(' \\r\\n')\n 817 |             if not row: continue\n 818 | \n 819 |             u_row = row.upper()\n 820 | \n 821 |             # DESCRIPTION -----------------------------------------------------\n 822 |             if u_row.startswith(_TK_DESCRIPTION) and STATE == _TK_DESCRIPTION:\n 823 |                 obj['description'] += self._decode_comment(row) + '\\n'\n 824 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 825 | \n 826 |             # RELATION --------------------------------------------------------\n 827 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_RELATION):\n 828 |                 if STATE != _TK_DESCRIPTION:\n 829 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 830 | \n 831 |                 STATE = _TK_RELATION\n 832 |                 obj['relation'] = self._decode_relation(row)\n 833 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 834 | \n 835 |             # ATTRIBUTE -------------------------------------------------------\n 836 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_ATTRIBUTE):\n 837 |                 if STATE != _TK_RELATION and STATE != _TK_ATTRIBUTE:\n 838 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 839 | \n 840 |                 STATE = _TK_ATTRIBUTE\n 841 | \n 842 |                 attr = self._decode_attribute(row)\n 843 |                 if attr[0] in attribute_names:\n 844 |                     raise BadAttributeName(attr[0], attribute_names[attr[0]])\n 845 |                 else:\n 846 |                     attribute_names[attr[0]] = self._current_line\n 847 |                 obj['attributes'].append(attr)\n 848 | \n 849 |                 if isinstance(attr[1], (list, tuple)):\n 850 |                     if encode_nominal:\n 851 |                         conversor = EncodedNominalConversor(attr[1])\n 852 |                     else:\n 853 |                         conversor = NominalConversor(attr[1])\n 854 |                 else:\n 855 |                     CONVERSOR_MAP = {'STRING': unicode,\n 856 |                                      'INTEGER': lambda x: int(float(x)),\n 857 |                                      'NUMERIC': float,\n 858 |                                      'REAL': float}\n 859 |                     conversor = CONVERSOR_MAP[attr[1]]\n 860 | \n 861 |                 self._conversors.append(conversor)\n 862 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 863 | \n 864 |             # DATA ------------------------------------------------------------\n 865 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_DATA):\n 866 |                 if STATE != _TK_ATTRIBUTE:\n 867 |                     raise BadLayout()\n 868 | \n 869 |                 break\n 870 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 871 | \n 872 |             # COMMENT ---------------------------------------------------------\n 873 |             elif u_row.startswith(_TK_COMMENT):\n 874 |                 pass\n 875 |             # -----------------------------------------------------------------\n 876 |         else:\n 877 |             # Never found @DATA\n 878 |             raise BadLayout()\n 879 | \n 880 |         def stream():\n 881 |             for row in s:\n 882 |                 self._current_line += 1\n 883 |                 row = row.strip()\n 884 |                 # Ignore empty lines and comment lines.\n 885 |                 if row and not row.startswith(_TK_COMMENT):\n 886 |                     yield row\n 887 | \n 888 |         # Alter the data object\n 889 |         obj['data'] = data.decode_rows(stream(), self._conversors)\n 890 |         if obj['description'].endswith('\\n'):\n 891 |             obj['description'] = obj['description'][:-1]\n 892 | \n 893 |         return obj\n 894 | \n 895 |     def decode(self, s, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n 896 |         '''Returns the Python representation of a given ARFF file.\n 897 | \n 898 |         When a file object is passed as an argument, this method reads lines\n 899 |         iteratively, avoiding to load unnecessary information to the memory.\n 900 | \n 901 |         :param s: a string or file object with the ARFF file.\n 902 |         :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n 903 |             while reading the .arff file.\n 904 |         :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n 905 |             dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n 906 |             `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n 907 |             Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n 908 |             progressively`_.\n 909 |         '''\n 910 |         try:\n 911 |             return self._decode(s, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n 912 |                                 matrix_type=return_type)\n 913 |         except ArffException as e:\n 914 |             e.line = self._current_line\n 915 |             raise e\n 916 | \n 917 | \n 918 | class ArffEncoder(object):\n 919 |     '''An ARFF encoder.'''\n 920 | \n 921 |     def _encode_comment(self, s=''):\n 922 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes a comment line.\n 923 | \n 924 |         Comments are single line strings starting, obligatorily, with the ``%``\n 925 |         character, and can have any symbol, including whitespaces or special\n 926 |         characters.\n 927 | \n 928 |         If ``s`` is None, this method will simply return an empty comment.\n 929 | \n 930 |         :param s: (OPTIONAL) string.\n 931 |         :return: a string with the encoded comment line.\n 932 |         '''\n 933 |         if s:\n 934 |             return u'%s %s'%(_TK_COMMENT, s)\n 935 |         else:\n 936 |             return u'%s' % _TK_COMMENT\n 937 | \n 938 |     def _encode_relation(self, name):\n 939 |         '''(INTERNAL) Decodes a relation line.\n 940 | \n 941 |         The relation declaration is a line with the format ``@RELATION\n 942 |         <relation-name>``, where ``relation-name`` is a string.\n 943 | \n 944 |         :param name: a string.\n 945 |         :return: a string with the encoded relation declaration.\n 946 |         '''\n 947 |         for char in ' %{},':\n 948 |             if char in name:\n 949 |                 name = '\"%s\"'%name\n 950 |                 break\n 951 | \n 952 |         return u'%s %s'%(_TK_RELATION, name)\n 953 | \n 954 |     def _encode_attribute(self, name, type_):\n 955 |         '''(INTERNAL) Encodes an attribute line.\n 956 | \n 957 |         The attribute follow the template::\n 958 | \n 959 |              @attribute <attribute-name> <datatype>\n 960 | \n 961 |         where ``attribute-name`` is a string, and ``datatype`` can be:\n 962 | \n 963 |         - Numerical attributes as ``NUMERIC``, ``INTEGER`` or ``REAL``.\n 964 |         - Strings as ``STRING``.\n 965 |         - Dates (NOT IMPLEMENTED).\n 966 |         - Nominal attributes with format:\n 967 | \n 968 |             {<nominal-name1>, <nominal-name2>, <nominal-name3>, ...}\n 969 | \n 970 |         This method must receive a the name of the attribute and its type, if\n 971 |         the attribute type is nominal, ``type`` must be a list of values.\n 972 | \n 973 |         :param name: a string.\n 974 |         :param type_: a string or a list of string.\n 975 |         :return: a string with the encoded attribute declaration.\n 976 |         '''\n 977 |         for char in ' %{},':\n 978 |             if char in name:\n 979 |                 name = '\"%s\"'%name\n 980 |                 break\n 981 | \n 982 |         if isinstance(type_, (tuple, list)):\n 983 |             type_tmp = [u'%s' % encode_string(type_k) for type_k in type_]\n 984 |             type_ = u'{%s}'%(u', '.join(type_tmp))\n 985 | \n 986 |         return u'%s %s %s'%(_TK_ATTRIBUTE, name, type_)\n 987 | \n 988 |     def encode(self, obj):\n 989 |         '''Encodes a given object to an ARFF file.\n 990 | \n 991 |         :param obj: the object containing the ARFF information.\n 992 |         :return: the ARFF file as an unicode string.\n 993 |         '''\n 994 |         data = [row for row in self.iter_encode(obj)]\n 995 | \n 996 |         return u'\\n'.join(data)\n 997 | \n 998 |     def iter_encode(self, obj):\n 999 |         '''The iterative version of `arff.ArffEncoder.encode`.\n1000 | \n1001 |         This encodes iteratively a given object and return, one-by-one, the\n1002 |         lines of the ARFF file.\n1003 | \n1004 |         :param obj: the object containing the ARFF information.\n1005 |         :return: (yields) the ARFF file as unicode strings.\n1006 |         '''\n1007 |         # DESCRIPTION\n1008 |         if obj.get('description', None):\n1009 |             for row in obj['description'].split('\\n'):\n1010 |                 yield self._encode_comment(row)\n1011 | \n1012 |         # RELATION\n1013 |         if not obj.get('relation'):\n1014 |             raise BadObject('Relation name not found or with invalid value.')\n1015 | \n1016 |         yield self._encode_relation(obj['relation'])\n1017 |         yield u''\n1018 | \n1019 |         # ATTRIBUTES\n1020 |         if not obj.get('attributes'):\n1021 |             raise BadObject('Attributes not found.')\n1022 | \n1023 |         attribute_names = set()\n1024 |         for attr in obj['attributes']:\n1025 |             # Verify for bad object format\n1026 |             if not isinstance(attr, (tuple, list)) or \\\n1027 |                len(attr) != 2 or \\\n1028 |                not isinstance(attr[0], basestring):\n1029 |                 raise BadObject('Invalid attribute declaration \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1030 | \n1031 |             if isinstance(attr[1], basestring):\n1032 |                 # Verify for invalid types\n1033 |                 if attr[1] not in _SIMPLE_TYPES:\n1034 |                     raise BadObject('Invalid attribute type \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1035 | \n1036 |             # Verify for bad object format\n1037 |             elif not isinstance(attr[1], (tuple, list)):\n1038 |                 raise BadObject('Invalid attribute type \"%s\"'%str(attr))\n1039 | \n1040 |             # Verify attribute name is not used twice\n1041 |             if attr[0] in attribute_names:\n1042 |                 raise BadObject('Trying to use attribute name \"%s\" for the '\n1043 |                                 'second time.' % str(attr[0]))\n1044 |             else:\n1045 |                 attribute_names.add(attr[0])\n1046 | \n1047 |             yield self._encode_attribute(attr[0], attr[1])\n1048 |         yield u''\n1049 |         attributes = obj['attributes']\n1050 | \n1051 |         # DATA\n1052 |         yield _TK_DATA\n1053 |         if 'data' in obj:\n1054 |             data = _get_data_object_for_encoding(obj.get('data'))\n1055 |             for line in data.encode_data(obj.get('data'), attributes):\n1056 |                 yield line\n1057 | \n1058 |         yield u''\n1059 | \n1060 | # =============================================================================\n1061 | \n1062 | # BASIC INTERFACE =============================================================\n1063 | def load(fp, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1064 |     '''Load a file-like object containing the ARFF document and convert it into\n1065 |     a Python object.\n1066 | \n1067 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1068 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1069 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1070 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1071 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1072 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1073 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1074 |         progressively`_.\n1075 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1076 |      '''\n1077 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1078 |     return decoder.decode(fp, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1079 |                           return_type=return_type)\n1080 | \n1081 | def loads(s, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1082 |     '''Convert a string instance containing the ARFF document into a Python\n1083 |     object.\n1084 | \n1085 |     :param s: a string object.\n1086 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1087 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1088 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1089 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1090 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1091 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1092 |         progressively`_.\n1093 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1094 |     '''\n1095 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1096 |     return decoder.decode(s, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1097 |                           return_type=return_type)\n1098 | \n1099 | def dump(obj, fp):\n1100 |     '''Serialize an object representing the ARFF document to a given file-like\n1101 |     object.\n1102 | \n1103 |     :param obj: a dictionary.\n1104 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1105 |     '''\n1106 |     encoder = ArffEncoder()\n1107 |     generator = encoder.iter_encode(obj)\n1108 | \n1109 |     last_row = next(generator)\n1110 |     for row in generator:\n1111 |         fp.write(last_row + u'\\n')\n1112 |         last_row = row\n1113 |     fp.write(last_row)\n1114 | \n1115 |     return fp\n1116 | \n1117 | def dumps(obj):\n1118 |     '''Serialize an object representing the ARFF document, returning a string.\n1119 | \n1120 |     :param obj: a dictionary.\n1121 |     :return: a string with the ARFF document.\n1122 |     '''\n1123 |     encoder = ArffEncoder()\n1124 |     return encoder.encode(obj)\n1125 | # =============================================================================\n1126 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis, I'll now localize the relevant code areas for implementing MD5 checksum validation during ARFF file download. Here's the structured output:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 840-841\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\ndescription: Main function that downloads the data and needs MD5 checksum validation integration\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 488-551\nfunction: _load_arff_response\ndescription: Function that handles loading the ARFF response and where checksum verification should be implemented\n\nsklearn/externals/_arff.py\nline: 151-153\ndescription: Import section where we can add hashlib import\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 809-811\nfunction: fetch_openml\ndescription: Where data_home and cache parameters are handled, where we should add the optional checksum validation flag\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 1\ndescription: Module where we can add utility functions for checksum validation\n```\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 85520, "completion_tokens": 430}, "time": 82.33808398246765}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30327", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py", "sklearn/base.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_0_22_0.py:test_sklearn_compatible_estimator", "sklearn/gaussian_process/kernels.py:Kernel", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/ensemble/_bagging.py:BaseBagging", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "sklearn/exceptions.py:EstimatorCheckFailedWarning.__str__", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark.make_scorers", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/linear_model.py:LassoBenchmark.skip", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit.symmetrize_raw_prediction", "sklearn/_loss/link.py:MultinomialLogit.link", "examples/release_highlights/plot_release_highlights_0_22_0.py:test_sklearn_compatible_estimator", "sklearn/gaussian_process/kernels.py:Kernel.n_dims", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:default_tags", "sklearn/ensemble/_bagging.py:BaseBagging._parallel_args", "sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I can now provide the locations that need to be modified to implement the old-new tag conversions. Here's the output in the requested format:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 360-433\nfunction: get_tags\n# This is the main function that needs to be modified to handle tag conversion\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 247-290\nfunction: default_tags\n# This function needs modification to support tag conversion when __sklearn_tags__ is not present\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 392-399\nfunction: BaseEstimator.__sklearn_tags__\n# This is where new tags are defined, needs to be considered for compatibility\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 457-515\nfunction: _to_new_tags\n# New function to be implemented for converting old tags to new format\n\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 519-583\nfunction: _to_old_tags\n# New function to be implemented for converting new tags to old format\n```\n\nThe main changes needed are:\n\n1. In `get_tags()`, the function needs to detect if the estimator implements `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` and convert accordingly.\n\n2. The `_to_new_tags()` function needs to:\n   - Detect if the estimator uses BaseEstimator's tag system\n   - Convert old tags to new format when present\n   - Raise warning if only BaseEstimator is used\n\n3. The `_to_old_tags()` function needs to:\n   - Check for both old and new tag formats\n   - Convert to old format when possible\n   - Handle cases where conversion isn't possible due to BaseEstimator-only implementation\n\n4. The `default_tags()` function needs to be updated to work with both old and new tag systems.\n\n5. Deprecation warnings should be added for `_safe_tags` and any related functionality that relies on it.\n\nThese modifications will enable:\n- Conversion between old and new tag systems\n- Warning users when estimator doesn't implement the full tag system\n- Proper handling of BaseEstimator-only implementations\n- Maintaining backward compatibility while transitioning to the new tag system"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "90a51c12d6159f72ab8990ea1a3ff835861ec81d", "problem_statement": "MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\r\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/base.py b/sklearn/base.py\nindex d646f8d3e56bf..2c82cf05a6c5a 100644\n--- a/sklearn/base.py\n+++ b/sklearn/base.py\n@@ -389,6 +389,33 @@ def __setstate__(self, state):\n         except AttributeError:\n             self.__dict__.update(state)\n \n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _more_tags(self):\n+        \"\"\"This code should never be reached since our `get_tags` will fallback on\n+        `__sklearn_tags__` implemented below. We keep it for backward compatibility.\n+        It is tested in `test_base_estimator_more_tags` in\n+        `sklearn/utils/testing/test_tags.py`.\"\"\"\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, default_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_more_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+        return _to_old_tags(default_tags(self))\n+\n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _get_tags(self):\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, get_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_get_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+\n+        return _to_old_tags(get_tags(self))\n+\n     def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n         return Tags(\n             estimator_type=None,\ndiff --git a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\nindex ccbc9d2438268..1ba1913c37234 100644\n--- a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n+++ b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import warnings\n+from collections import OrderedDict\n from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from itertools import chain\n \n from .fixes import _dataclass_args\n \n@@ -290,6 +292,71 @@ def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     )\n \n \n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _find_tags_provider(estimator, warn=True):\n+    \"\"\"Find the tags provider for an estimator.\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    estimator : estimator object\n+        The estimator to find the tags provider for.\n+\n+    warn : bool, default=True\n+        Whether to warn if the tags provider is not found.\n+\n+    Returns\n+    -------\n+    tag_provider : str\n+        The tags provider for the estimator. Can be one of:\n+        - \"_get_tags\": to use the old tags infrastructure\n+        - \"__sklearn_tags__\": to use the new tags infrastructure\n+    \"\"\"\n+    mro_model = type(estimator).mro()\n+    tags_mro = OrderedDict()\n+    for klass in mro_model:\n+        tags_provider = []\n+        if \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_more_tags\")\n+        if \"_get_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_get_tags\")\n+        if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"__sklearn_tags__\")\n+        tags_mro[klass.__name__] = tags_provider\n+\n+    all_providers = set(chain.from_iterable(tags_mro.values()))\n+    if \"__sklearn_tags__\" not in all_providers:\n+        # default on the old tags infrastructure\n+        return \"_get_tags\"\n+\n+    tag_provider = \"__sklearn_tags__\"\n+    for klass in tags_mro:\n+        has_get_or_more_tags = any(\n+            provider in tags_mro[klass] for provider in (\"_get_tags\", \"_more_tags\")\n+        )\n+        has_sklearn_tags = \"__sklearn_tags__\" in tags_mro[klass]\n+\n+        if tags_mro[klass] and tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":  # is it empty\n+            if has_get_or_more_tags and not has_sklearn_tags:\n+                # Case where a class does not implement __sklearn_tags__ and we fallback\n+                # to _get_tags. We should therefore warn for implementing\n+                # __sklearn_tags__.\n+                tag_provider = \"_get_tags\"\n+                break\n+\n+    if warn and tag_provider == \"_get_tags\":\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            f\"The {estimator.__class__.__name__} or classes from which it inherits \"\n+            \"use `_get_tags` and `_more_tags`. Please define the \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` method, or inherit from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` \"\n+            \"and/or other appropriate mixins such as `sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, `sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`. From scikit-learn 1.7, not defining \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` will raise an error.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+    return tag_provider\n+\n+\n def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n \n@@ -316,19 +383,201 @@ def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n         The estimator tags.\n     \"\"\"\n \n-    if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n+    tag_provider = _find_tags_provider(estimator)\n+\n+    if tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":\n         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n     else:\n-        warnings.warn(\n-            f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n-            \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n-            \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n-            \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n-            category=FutureWarning,\n+        # TODO(1.7): Remove this branch of the code\n+        # Let's go through the MRO and patch each class implementing _more_tags\n+        sklearn_tags_provider = {}\n+        more_tags_provider = {}\n+        class_order = []\n+        for klass in reversed(type(estimator).mro()):\n+            if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+                sklearn_tags_provider[klass] = klass.__sklearn_tags__(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+            elif \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+                more_tags_provider[klass] = klass._more_tags(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+\n+        # Find differences between consecutive in the case of __sklearn_tags__\n+        # inheritance\n+        sklearn_tags_diff = {}\n+        items = list(sklearn_tags_provider.items())\n+        for current_item, next_item in zip(items[:-1], items[1:]):\n+            current_name, current_tags = current_item\n+            next_name, next_tags = next_item\n+            current_tags = _to_old_tags(current_tags)\n+            next_tags = _to_old_tags(next_tags)\n+\n+            # Compare tags and store differences\n+            diff = {}\n+            for key in current_tags:\n+                if current_tags[key] != next_tags[key]:\n+                    diff[key] = next_tags[key]\n+\n+            sklearn_tags_diff[next_name] = diff\n+\n+        tags = {}\n+        for klass in class_order:\n+            if klass in sklearn_tags_diff:\n+                tags.update(sklearn_tags_diff[klass])\n+            elif klass in more_tags_provider:\n+                tags.update(more_tags_provider[klass])\n+\n+        tags = _to_new_tags(\n+            {**_to_old_tags(default_tags(estimator)), **tags}, estimator\n         )\n-        tags = default_tags(estimator)\n \n     return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _safe_tags(estimator, key=None):\n+    warnings.warn(\n+        \"The `_safe_tags` function is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+        \"1.7. Use the public `get_tags` function instead and make sure to implement \"\n+        \"the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+        category=FutureWarning,\n+    )\n+    tags = _to_old_tags(get_tags(estimator))\n+\n+    if key is not None:\n+        if key not in tags:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                f\"The key {key} is not defined for the class \"\n+                f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__}.\"\n+            )\n+        return tags[key]\n+    return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_new_tags(old_tags, estimator=None):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    input_tags = InputTags(\n+        one_d_array=\"1darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_array=\"2darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        three_d_array=\"3darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        sparse=\"sparse\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        categorical=\"categorical\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        string=\"string\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        dict=\"dict\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_X\"],\n+        allow_nan=old_tags[\"allow_nan\"],\n+        pairwise=old_tags[\"pairwise\"],\n+    )\n+    target_tags = TargetTags(\n+        required=old_tags[\"requires_y\"],\n+        one_d_labels=\"1dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_labels=\"2dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_y\"],\n+        multi_output=old_tags[\"multioutput\"] or old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+        single_output=not old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+    )\n+    if estimator is not None and (\n+        hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n+    ):\n+        transformer_tags = TransformerTags(\n+            preserves_dtype=old_tags[\"preserves_dtype\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        transformer_tags = None\n+    estimator_type = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None)\n+    if estimator_type == \"classifier\":\n+        classifier_tags = ClassifierTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_class=not old_tags[\"binary_only\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        classifier_tags = None\n+    if estimator_type == \"regressor\":\n+        regressor_tags = RegressorTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        regressor_tags = None\n+    return Tags(\n+        estimator_type=estimator_type,\n+        target_tags=target_tags,\n+        transformer_tags=transformer_tags,\n+        classifier_tags=classifier_tags,\n+        regressor_tags=regressor_tags,\n+        input_tags=input_tags,\n+        array_api_support=old_tags[\"array_api_support\"],\n+        no_validation=old_tags[\"no_validation\"],\n+        non_deterministic=old_tags[\"non_deterministic\"],\n+        requires_fit=old_tags[\"requires_fit\"],\n+        _skip_test=old_tags[\"_skip_test\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_old_tags(new_tags):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    if new_tags.classifier_tags:\n+        binary_only = not new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_class\n+        multilabel_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        binary_only = False\n+        multilabel_clf = False\n+        poor_score_clf = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.regressor_tags:\n+        multilabel_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        multilabel_reg = False\n+        poor_score_reg = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.transformer_tags:\n+        preserves_dtype = new_tags.transformer_tags.preserves_dtype\n+    else:\n+        preserves_dtype = [\"float64\"]\n+\n+    tags = {\n+        \"allow_nan\": new_tags.input_tags.allow_nan,\n+        \"array_api_support\": new_tags.array_api_support,\n+        \"binary_only\": binary_only,\n+        \"multilabel\": multilabel_clf or multilabel_reg,\n+        \"multioutput\": new_tags.target_tags.multi_output,\n+        \"multioutput_only\": (\n+            not new_tags.target_tags.single_output and new_tags.target_tags.multi_output\n+        ),\n+        \"no_validation\": new_tags.no_validation,\n+        \"non_deterministic\": new_tags.non_deterministic,\n+        \"pairwise\": new_tags.input_tags.pairwise,\n+        \"preserves_dtype\": preserves_dtype,\n+        \"poor_score\": poor_score_clf or poor_score_reg,\n+        \"requires_fit\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+        \"requires_positive_X\": new_tags.input_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"requires_y\": new_tags.target_tags.required,\n+        \"requires_positive_y\": new_tags.target_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"_skip_test\": new_tags._skip_test,\n+        \"stateless\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+    }\n+    X_types = []\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.one_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"1darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.two_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"2darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.three_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"3darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.sparse:\n+        X_types.append(\"sparse\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.categorical:\n+        X_types.append(\"categorical\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.string:\n+        X_types.append(\"string\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.dict:\n+        X_types.append(\"dict\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.one_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"1dlabels\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.two_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"2dlabels\")\n+    tags[\"X_types\"] = X_types\n+    return tags\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 96400, "completion_tokens": 741}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - Use the unique artifacts provided to you to complement your understanding of the execution flow, or use as a hint.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\n\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style analysis):\n# Unique Artifacts\n\nUse these unique artifacts as supplemental hints, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace A role: `feature_positive`\n- Trace B role: `feature_negative`\n- Comparison: `trace_a_minus_trace_b`\n- Unique functions in JSON: `9`\n- Unique functions shown here: `9`\n- Target package filter: `sklearn`\n\n## Related Files\n- `sklearn/utils/_tags.py`: `8` unique function(s)\n- `sklearn/base.py`: `1` unique function(s)\n\n## Artifact Candidates\n- artifact: `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:__init__`\n  - module: `sklearn.utils._tags`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 3`\n  - observed lines: `3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, ...`\n  - trace activity: 99 events, 9 calls, 81 line events, 9 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 4\n- artifact: `sklearn/utils/_tags.py:_find_tags_provider`\n  - module: `sklearn.utils._tags`\n  - definition: `lines 296-357`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 314`\n  - observed lines: `314, 315, 316, 317, 318, 319, 320, 321, ...`\n  - trace activity: 57 events, 1 calls, 55 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 2\n  - sample executed li