', 'values': use the actual numerical values of the index.\n1013 |             * 'nearest', 'zero', 'slinear', 'quadratic', 'cubic', 'barycentric',\n1014 |             'polynomial': Passed to scipy.interpolate.interp1d, whereas 'spline'\n1015 |             is passed to scipy.interpolate.UnivariateSpline. These methods use\n1016 |             the numerical values of the index.\n1017 |             Both 'polynomial' and 'spline' require that you also specify an\n1018 |             order (int), e.g. arr.interpolate(method='polynomial', order=5).\n1019 |             * 'krogh', 'piecewise_polynomial', 'spline', 'pchip', 'akima',\n1020 |             'cubicspline': Wrappers around the SciPy interpolation methods\n1021 |             of similar names. See Notes.\n1022 |             * 'from_derivatives': Refers to scipy.interpolate.BPoly.from_derivatives.\n1023 |         axis : int\n1024 |             Axis to interpolate along. For 1-dimensional data, use 0.\n1025 |         index : Index\n1026 |             Index to use for interpolation.\n1027 |         limit : int or None\n1028 |             Maximum number of consecutive NaNs to fill. Must be greater than 0.\n1029 |         limit_direction : {'forward', 'backward', 'both'}\n1030 |             Consecutive NaNs will be filled in this direction.\n1031 |         limit_area : {'inside', 'outside'} or None\n1032 |             If limit is specified, consecutive NaNs will be filled with this\n1033 |             restriction.\n1034 |             * None: No fill restriction.\n1035 |             * 'inside': Only fill NaNs surrounded by valid values (interpolate).\n1036 |             * 'outside': Only fill NaNs outside valid values (extrapolate).\n1037 |         copy : bool\n1038 |             If True, a copy of the object is returned with interpolated values.\n1039 |         **kwargs : optional\n1040 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to the interpolating function.\n1041 | \n1042 |         Returns\n1043 |         -------\n1044 |