-zag chains of hexagons; see the\n5014 |             following illustration.\n5015 | \n5016 |             .. plot::\n5017 | \n5018 |                import numpy\n5019 |                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n5020 | \n5021 |                np.random.seed(19680801)\n5022 |                n= 300\n5023 |                x = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5024 |                y = np.random.standard_normal(n)\n5025 | \n5026 |                fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(4, 4))\n5027 |                h = ax.hexbin(x, y, gridsize=(5, 3))\n5028 |                hx, hy = h.get_offsets().T\n5029 |                ax.plot(hx[24::3], hy[24::3], 'ro-')\n5030 |                ax.plot(hx[-3:], hy[-3:], 'ro-')\n5031 |                ax.set_title('gridsize=(5, 3)')\n5032 |                ax.axis('off')\n5033 | \n5034 |             To get approximately regular hexagons, choose\n5035 |             :math:`n_x = \\\\sqrt{3}\\\\,n_y`.\n5036 | \n5037 |         bins : 'log' or int or sequence, default: None\n5038 |             Discretization of the hexagon values.\n5039 | \n5040 |             - If *None*, no binning is applied; the color of each hexagon\n5041 |               directly corresponds to its count value.\n5042 |             - If 'log', use a logarithmic scale for the colormap.\n5043 |               Internally, :math:`log_{10}(i+1)` is used to determine the\n5044 |               hexagon color. This is equivalent to ``norm=LogNorm()``.\n5045 |             - If an integer, divide the counts in the specified number\n5046 |               of bins, and color the hexagons accordingly.\n5047 |             - If a sequence of values, the values of the lower bound of\n5048 |               the bins to be used.\n5049 | \n5050 |         xscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5051 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the horizontal axis.\n5052 | \n5053 |         yscale : {'linear', 'log'}, default: 'linear'\n5054 |             Use a linear or log10 scale on the vertical axis.\n5055 | \n5056 |         mincnt : int >= 0, default: *None*\n5057 |             If not *None*, only display cells with at least *mincnt*\n5058 |             number of points in the cell.\n5059 | \n5060 |         marginals : bool, default: *False*\n5061 |             If marginals is *True*, plot the marginal density as\n5062 |             colormapped rectangles along the bottom of the x-axis and\n5063 |             left of the y-axis.\n5064 | \n5065 |         extent : 4-tuple of float, default: *None*\n5066 |             The limits of the bins (xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax).\n5067 |             The default assigns the limits based on\n5068 |             *gridsize*, *x*, *y*, *xscale* and *yscale*.\n5069 | \n5070 |             If *xscale* or *yscale* is set to 'log', the limits are\n5071 |             expected to be the exponent for a power of 10. E.g. for\n5072 |             x-limits of 1 and 50 in 'linear' scale and y-limits\n5073 |             of 10 and 1000 in 'log' scale, enter (1, 50, 1, 3).\n5074 | \n5075 |         Returns\n5076 |         -------\n5077 |         `~matplotlib.collections.PolyCollection`\n5078 |             A `.PolyCollection` defining the hexagonal bins.\n5079 | \n5080 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_offsets` contains a Mx2 array containing\n5081 |               the x, y positions of the M hexagon centers in data coordinates.\n5082 |             - `.PolyCollection.get_array` contains the values of the M\n5083 |               hexagons.\n5084 | \n5085 |             If *marginals* is *True*, horizontal\n5086 |             bar and vertical bar (both PolyCollections) will be attached\n5087 |             to the return collection as attributes *hbar* and *vbar*.\n5088 | \n5089 |         Other Parameters\n5090 |         ----------------\n5091 |         %(cmap_doc)s\n5092 | \n5093 |         %(norm_doc)s\n5094 | \n5095 |         %(vmin_vmax_doc)s\n5096 | \n5097 |         alpha : float between 0 and 1, optional\n5098 |             The alpha blending v